	// RevokeTokenWithBodyWithResponse Sign an app's person out by handing back its refresh token
	//
	// THE REVOCATION ENDPOINT (RFC 7009; architecture decision record 037 rule Eight). An app signing
	// its person out sends the refresh token `requestTokens` answered it, with its `client_id`, and
	// identity ends the token's whole family: every refresh token since the code, and every session
	// they came with. Every Onplo service refuses those sessions from the next call on, because
	// revocation is read on every call (rule Two). The person's other apps stay signed in.
	//
	// ANSWERED 200 WITH NOTHING TO SAY, also for a token identity does not hold or already ended
	// (RFC 7009 §2.2): the app throws its tokens away either way. A token shown by another app is
	// `invalid_client`, and nothing ends. An access token is not taken here
	// (`token_type_hint=access_token` with no refresh token of ours is `unsupported_token_type`): it
	// ends with its family when the refresh token is handed back.
	//
	// SIGNED OUT, because the refresh token is the proof, and LIMITED by how often it is called
	// (decision 0732).
	//
	// Takes any type of body and a specified content type, and returns a wrapper object for the known response body format(s).
	//
	// Corresponds with POST /revoke (the `RevokeToken` operationId).
